/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  TWO OUTLETS.  A flash drum has a vapour line at the top and a bottoms line
  at the bottom -- that is the equipment, and the topology describes the
  equipment.

  An earlier draft of this file declared FIVE outlets, one per phase the
  solver resolves inside the vessel.  That confused what the SOLVER computes
  with what the EQUIPMENT has.  The five phases are real and every one of
  them is reported, but they leave through two nozzles: the vapour, and the
  bottoms carrying the aqueous liquid, the organic liquid and both solids
  together.

  Whoever needs them apart puts a decanter and a filter downstream, and those
  are units with their own records -- not extra nozzles on a drum.  The
  internal phase split is a RESULT, reported in the bottoms stream's own
  file; it is not topology.

  NO NEW UNIT TYPE (settled 2026-07-27).  A flash holds T and P and splits
  into whatever phases the declared thermo world admits.  A
  `multiphaseReactiveFlash` would force gatekeeping classifications -- which
  components "may" enter which flash -- and give the student two flashes to
  learn instead of one.  The phase repertoire grows in the formulation
  declared by constant/thermoPhysPropDict, never in the unit.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        flash01;
        type        isothermalFlash;

        in          feed;
        outputs     ( bottoms  vapour );

        operation   { T 308.15 K;  P 2.0 atm; }
    }
);
